    "rich_text_kiMLrU": {
      "type": "rich-text",
      "settings": {
        "title": "Simulated Process Printing: Achieving Photorealism with Fewer Colors",
        "heading_size": "heading-standard",
        "rich_text": "<h3><strong>Mastering the Art of Color Blending</strong></h3><p>Simulated process printing, often referred to as \"sim process,\" is a sophisticated screen printing technique that allows us to achieve stunning, photorealistic results with a limited number of colors. Unlike traditional 4-color process printing (CMYK), which relies on four primary colors, simulated process utilizes a carefully selected palette of spot colors, typically ranging from six to twelve, to create a wide range of hues and shades.</p>",
        "display_on_card": true,
        "content_width_desktop": "lg:w-2/3",
        "button_link": "",
        "button_label": "Shop Now",
        "color_scheme": "primary",
        "background_override": "",
        "use_gradient_background": false
      }
    },
    "image_with_text_XK368A": {
      "type": "image-with-text",
      "settings": {
        "display_on_card": true,
        "image": "shopify://shop_images/AdobeStock_1108966511.jpg",
        "crop": "",
        "image_shape": "blob",
        "blob_type": 1,
        "rotate_image": 0,
        "image_position": "right",
        "image_size": "half",
        "disable_media_style": false,
        "title": "The Art of Color Blending",
        "heading_size": "heading-standard",
        "richtext": "<p>Simulated process leverages the principle of halftoning, where tiny dots of varying sizes are used to create the illusion of continuous tone. By strategically combining these halftone dots of different spot colors, our skilled technicians can achieve remarkable color blending and create a wide spectrum of hues, mimicking the appearance of a full-color image.</p>",
        "text_alignment": "text-left",
        "button_link": "",
        "button_label": "Shop Now",
        "color_scheme": "primary",
        "use_gradient_background": false
      }
    },
